Segregate even and odd nodes in a Linked List - GeeksforGeeks

Given a singly linked list, rearrange the list in place so that all nodes containing even values appear before nodes containing odd values. Return the head of the modified linked list. Note: The relative order of the even nodes and the odd nodes should remain unchanged.

Examples: Input:  Output: 8 -> 2 -> 4 -> 6 -> 17 -> 15 -> 9 Explanation: Since 8, 2, 4, and 6 are even, they appear first in the list, followed by the odd numbers 17, 15, and 9. Input:  Output: 1 -> 3 -> 5 -> 7 Explanation: There are no even numbers in the list.

The Idea is to traverse the list and extract all even nodes while maintaining order. Build a separate even list and finally append the remaining odd nodes. 

This problem can be solved using a single pass through the linked list. We can use a temporary list to store the even nodes and then append the odd nodes to the end of the list.

Here is a sample code in Java to solve this problem:

```java
public class Node {
    int data;
    Node next;
    public Node(int d) {
        data = d;
        next = null;
    }
}

public class GFG {
    public static Node divide(Node head) {
        // Node to store the even list
        Node evenHead = null;
        Node evenTail = null;

        // Node to store the odd list
        Node oddHead = null;
        Node oddTail = null;

        // Traverse the linked list
        while (head != null) {
            // If the current node is even
            if (head.data % 2 == 0) {
                // Add it to the even list
                if (evenHead == null) {
                    evenHead = head;
                    evenTail = head;
                } else {
                    evenTail.next = head;
                    evenTail = head;
                }
            } else {
                // Add it to the odd list
                if (oddHead == null) {
                    oddHead = head;
                    oddTail = head;
                } else {
                    oddTail.next = head;
                    oddTail = head;
                }
            }
            // Move to the next node
            head = head.next;
        }

        // If the even list is not empty, append the odd list to it
        if (evenHead != null) {
            evenTail.next = oddHead;
        }

        // Return the head of the modified linked list
        return evenHead;
    }

    public static void printList(Node node) {
        while (node != null) {
            System.out.print(node.data + " ");
            node = node.next;
        }
        System.out.println();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Node head = new Node(8);
        head.next = new Node(2);
        head.next.next = new Node(4);
        head.next.next.next = new Node(6);
        head.next.next.next.next = new Node(17);
        head.next.next.next.next.next = new Node(15);
        head.next.next.next.next.next.next = new Node(9);

        System.out.println("Original Linked List: ");
        printList(head);

        head = divide(head);

        System.out.println("Modified Linked List: ");
        printList(head);
    }
}
